This Jenkins task represents a real-world DevOps automation scenario: instead of manually logging into servers and installing software, a team creates a Jenkins job that can install packages automatically and consistently.

Real-world example

Imagine a company has 50 Linux servers running applications. A security team discovers that all servers need a new package installed:

vim-enhanced (example)
a monitoring agent
a security patch
a database client
a backup tool
a new application dependency

Without automation, an administrator would have to:

SSH into every server.
Run the installation command.
Enter passwords.
Verify installation.
Repeat 50 times.

This is slow and error-prone.

With Jenkins automation:

A DevOps engineer creates a Jenkins job:
install-packages
The job accepts a parameter:
PACKAGE

Example:

PACKAGE=nodejs
Jenkins connects to the required servers:
ststor01
app-server-01
app-server-02
Jenkins runs:
sudo dnf install -y nodejs
Jenkins reports success/failure.
